}}|subdivision_type1=[[Communities of Balisca|Community]]|subdivision_name1={{flag|Araucaria}}|area_km2=13857|population_census=4,759,045|population_census_year=2025|regional_languages=|common_languages=[[Mozarabic]], [[Baliscano]], [[Maroniko language|Maroniko]] and [[Kurupiran language|Kurupirangüe]]|cctld=.bra|ethnic_groups=82.4% [[Baliscans]] <br> 17.6% others|GDP_PPP=$230.226 billion|GDP_PPP_per_capita=$48,376|GDP_PPP_year=2025|GDP_PPP_rank=8th|GDP_PPP_rank_link=List of Baliscan regions by GDP|GDP_PPP_per_capita_rank=5th|GDP_PPP_per_capita_rank_link=List of Baliscan regions by GDP|population_census_rank=13th}}'''Brasal''' (From [[Mesogean language|Mesogean]] ''{{Linktext|brasa}}''; {{Literal translation|ember|coal}}; {{Langx|tpn|Kûaray-ybyrá}}), is the most populous [[Regions of Balisca|region]] of [[Araucaria]], in [[Balisca]]. It borders [[Tukupi]] and [[Juyú]] to the south, the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] to the east, and [[Luzamontaña]] to the north. The administrative seat and most populous [[Communes of Balisca|commune]] of Brasal is [[Calabazas]]. 

The region's name originated from early Mesogean exploration, in reference to the distinctive dark reddish-brown coloration of heartwood from the ''[[W:Araucaria angustifolia|Araucaria angustifolia]]'' trees, caused by resinous tracheids that produced it's distinct reddish-brown dye. 

